 <h2 id="check">Check your understanding</h2>
 <p>One or more questions for each outcome at the top of the page; the quiz in <a href="#geometry">Reading the geometry</a> and Figures 2 and 3 cover more.</p>
 <section class="lesson quiz-lesson" data-lesson="section" aria-labelledby="section-quiz-title">
  <h3 id="section-quiz-title">Quiz: shots, sections and identification</h3>
  <p class="task" data-prompt aria-live="polite"></p>
  <div class="buttons" data-choices></div>
  <p class="feedback" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
  <div class="buttons"><button type="button" data-new>Next question</button></div>
 </section>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 1</span> A bandit at 20 NM shows 20 R target aspect, then turns to put you on its nose. What happened to your lateral separation?</summary><p>It went from about 40,000 feet to zero, instantly: TA is now 0, and LS = TA × range × 100.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 2</span> The contact holds the same bearing as the range closes. What does that tell you?</summary><p>You are on a collision course; with both aircraft at the same speed, your angle-off equals its target aspect, on the other side.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 2</span> You have 40,000 feet of lateral separation at 25 NM and want to keep it to 10 NM. What heading do you fly?</summary><p>The bandit reciprocal: on a zero cut, lateral separation does not change.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 3</span> Why might a fighter not shoot at maximum range, even with a valid launch zone?</summary><p>A long shot gives the bandit time to see it and maneuver, and the missile arrives with little energy, so it is easy to defeat kinematically; ROPT trades range for end-game energy.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 4</span> Your missile times out and the radar contact disappears. Was it a kill?</summary><p>Not necessarily: timeout only says the missile has reached its expected target. A lost contact after timeout may mean a kill.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 5</span> In what order does a section run its intercept tasks?</summary><p>Picture building, commit, label and name, target, meld, sort, shoot, decide, merge.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 6</span> Your jet has no bullseye readout. Steering to bullseye shows 110° at 30 NM, and a group is called at bullseye 290, 50. Where is it from you?</summary><p>You are on the reciprocal, bullseye 290, 30. The group is on the same bullseye bearing, so the ranges subtract: about 20 NM beyond you, bearing about 290° from you.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 6</span> What is the difference between a broadcast call and a tactical call about the same group?</summary><p>Broadcast is for everyone, in bullseye, with a track direction; tactical is for one flight, in bullseye or BRAA, with the group's aspect to that flight.</p></details>

 <h2 id="sources">Sources and applicability</h2>
 <h3>Military sources</h3>
 <p id="source-p825"><strong>[1] US Navy, Chief of Naval Air Training, CNATRA P-825 (07-14), Flight Training Instruction, Basic Fighter Maneuvering (BFM) and All Weather Intercept (AWI), Advanced UMFO T-45C/VMTS, 1 July 2014.</strong> <a href="https://falcon.blu3wolf.com/Docs/P-825.pdf">Read on a public copy</a>, 9 November 2026, by printed page: fighter missions and pursuit, pp. 4-2 to 4-4; air intercept control, pp. 5-2 to 5-9; intercept geometry, pp. 8-1 to 9-20; target aspect control and the stern conversion, pp. 10-1 to 10-12 and 13-10; missiles, pp. 14-4 to 14-7; missile employment, pp. 15-1 to 15-10; advanced intercepts, pp. 16-1 to 16-14; section employment, pp. 17-12 to 17-48; brevity codes, pp. A-1 to A-11. The Navy's training text for student naval flight officers and Marine Corps navigators; CNATRA's own site no longer serves it, and a 2017 revision exists that was not read. It teaches skills, not fleet tactics.</p>
 <p id="source-jp"><strong>[2] US Joint Chiefs of Staff, JP 3-01, Countering Air and Missile Threats, 23 March 2012.</strong> <a href="https://www.bits.de/NRANEU/others/jp-doctrine/jp3_01(12).pdf">Read on the Berlin Information-center for Transatlantic Security's copy</a>, 9 November 2026, pp. III-11 to III-17. The joint publication for all four services; a later edition exists that could not be read.</p>
 <h3>Sim sources</h3>
 <p id="source-f16"><strong>[3] Eagle Dynamics, DCS: F-16C Viper Early Access Guide (English).</strong> <a href="https://www.digitalcombatsimulator.com/en/downloads/documentation/">DCS documentation page</a>, read 9 November 2026, pp. 269–271, 356–357 and 595–596. A sim manual, cited only for the In the sim panel and Figure 4's shape.</p>
 <p id="source-bms"><strong>[4] Benchmark Sims, Falcon BMS 4.38.1 Training Manual.</strong> <a href="https://cdn.falcon-bms.com/docs/4.38/BMS-Training-Manual.pdf">PDF</a>, read 9 November 2026, pp. 256, 259–260, 295–297, 310 and 314. A sim manual, cited only for the In the sim panel.</p>
 <p id="source-f14"><strong>[5] Heatblur Simulations, DCS: F-14 Tomcat manual.</strong> <a href="https://f14.manuals.heatblur.se/print.html">Online manual</a>, read 10 November 2026, by section: Navigation Command and Control Grid (NAV GRID); HSD Mode Switch; Programmable Tactical Information Display. A sim manual, cited only for the In the sim panel.</p>
 <p>Page-specific excerpts are recorded in the repository review evidence: <a href="https://github.com/burnzoire/oversweep/blob/main/docs/reviews/bvr-intercepts-evidence.md">BVR intercepts evidence</a>.</p>
 <p>Figures 1 to 4 and the worked intercept are original, invented examples, not reproductions of any publication's figure.</p>
 <p class="note">Applicability: written for fighter aircrew of every service. The procedures are the Navy's published training standards, which it says are skills rather than tactics, with the Air Force's aspect conventions noted; no Air Force or Army intercept publication could be read. Identification is from joint doctrine. Real tactics, missile performance, theater rules of engagement, SPINS, your unit's procedures and the current editions govern.</p>
